# API Reference

[See the docs 📚](https://alexapy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/index.html).

## Smart-home device metadata

`await AlexaAPI.get_devices_gql(login)` returns the full smart-home endpoint
records. Each record can include `model` (`type` and `value.text`/`value.locale`),
`dmsIdentifier` (`deviceType` and `dsn`), and `legacyAppliance`, including its
`additionalApplianceDetails` JSON value.

Match the DMS device type and serial number to the REST device list's `deviceType`
and `serialNumber`. Model metadata may be null or sealed; only use `PLAIN` values
as readable descriptions. Amazon can provide descriptions such as
`Echo Studio (2025 release)` or `Echo (4th Gen)`, but does not guarantee a model
name or hardware generation for every device. A release year is not a numbered
generation. The structure of `additionalApplianceDetails` is device-dependent.

`get_network_details(login)` continues to return only legacy appliance records,
including `additionalApplianceDetails`. Call `get_devices_gql(login)` when the
sibling endpoint model and DMS identifier metadata are needed.

## Smart-home groups and devices

Smart-home groups are the rooms of the Alexa app. Their members are the
`applianceId` values of `get_network_details(login)`.

- `get_appliance_groups(login)` returns the group documents (`groupId`, `name`,
  `applianceIds`, ...).
- `create_appliance_group(login, name, appliance_ids=None)` creates a group and
  returns its document, including the new `groupId`.
- `set_appliance_group_members(login, group_id, appliance_ids)` replaces the
  members. `rename_appliance_group(login, group_id, name)` renames the group and
  keeps its members. Both read the group first and send the rest of its document
  back unchanged.
- `delete_appliance_group(login, group_id)` deletes the group, not its devices.
- `discover_appliances(login)` asks Alexa to search for new devices, like the
  device search of the Alexa app. Echo devices with a Zigbee hub search as well,
  so it can add devices that were not in the account before, without a group.

Device changes take the endpoint `id` of `get_devices_gql(login)`
(`amzn1.alexa.endpoint...`), not the `applianceId`:

- `set_endpoint_friendly_name(login, endpoint_id, name)` renames a device. Amazon
  rejects some names, for example names with an underscore.
- `set_endpoint_enabled(login, endpoint_id, enabled)` enables or disables a device.
- `forget_endpoint(login, endpoint_id)` deletes a device from the account.

The write methods return `True` when Amazon accepted the change.

## Echo devices and skills

- `rename_device(login, serial, device_type, device_account_id, name)` renames an
  Alexa device such as an Echo. The arguments are the `serialNumber`,
  `deviceType` and `deviceAccountId` of `get_devices(login)`.
- `get_skills(login)` returns the skills enabled for the account, each with
  `title`, `asin`, `productMetadata.skillId` and `productDetails.skillTypes`.

